ass: skip the blocks of events that cannot be active

ass_render_frame read Start and Duration of every event of the track to
find the few that are on screen. A track of frame-by-frame typesetting has
tens of thousands of events (36880 and 45106 in two episodes of the series
this was measured with), three megabytes of event structs walked for every
frame: 0.4 ms on a Fire TV Stick 4K Max's Cortex-A55 at full speed, more
at the frequency it idles at between frames. That is all a frame without
a change costs, and was 6.6% of the time the episode takes to render
there; such a frame now takes 0.1 ms.

The events array is now described in blocks of 64 events by the earliest
start and the latest end in the block, and a frame walks only the blocks
whose span holds it. The frame's events are the same, in the same order.

ASS_Track is a public struct and callers edit it: mpv settles the duration
of the newest events after the fact, fills events in after
ass_alloc_event, and drops old ones by moving the array down. So the
blocks are trusted only as far as this goes:
- an event is looked at before it is rendered, so a span that got shorter
  costs a look at its block and nothing else;
- the last two blocks are described again for every frame;
- events added or removed through libass update or void the blocks, and a
  count or an array that changed behind libass' back voids them, at the
  next ass_alloc_event or frame;
- a share of the older blocks is described again for every frame, so a
  write that extends or moves an old event without any of the above is
  honored after 32 frames at the latest.
Tracks of fewer than 512 events are walked as before.

scripts/test_libass_event_index.c renders through all of these and
compares every frame with the events that are active.

